id: rust.bounded-concurrency
kind: rust
title: Bounded concurrency
aliases: buffered, buffer_unordered, for_each_concurrent, fan out
tags: async, streams, backpressure
questions:
  - how do you process 10k items concurrently without running them all at once?
invariant: Concurrency is a limit you choose, so memory stays proportional to the limit rather than to the input.
answer_30s: Use a stream with buffered(n) or for_each_concurrent(Some(n)) so at most n items are in flight, which keeps memory flat while saturating the limit.
answer_2m: |
  Unbounded fan-out is the most common async bug in review: join_all,
  spawn-per-item and collect-then-wait all create as much work as there is
  input. buffered(n) preserves order and bounds in-flight futures;
  buffer_unordered(n) is faster when order does not matter. When the work is
  CPU-bound rather than IO-bound, the bound is spawn_blocking plus a Semaphore,
  because a blocked runtime worker is worse than a queued task.
when_to_use:
  - paging through a large collection with IO per item, upstream calls under a rate limit
avoid_when:
  - work that must be sequential end to end (use a single loop)
minimal_example: |
  use futures::stream::{self, StreamExt};

  let results: Vec<_> = stream::iter(urls)
      .map(|u| fetch(u))
      .buffered(8)   // at most 8 in flight, order preserved
      .collect()
      .await;
  let _ = results;

  // Order irrelevant: same bound, more throughput.
  // stream::iter(urls).map(fetch).buffer_unordered(8).collect::<Vec<_>>().await;
failure_modes:
  - join_all over a large collection, so memory grows with the input
  - choosing the bound from the database limit instead of measuring downstream capacity
related:
  - rust.tokio-semaphore
  - rust.async-streams
  - design.backpressure
  - design.worker-pool
  - design.retry-backoff
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/futures/latest/futures/stream/trait.StreamExt.html#method.buffered
